\title{A $q$-Analogue of Kummer's Theorem}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Kummer's theorem determines the exact power of a prime $p$ dividing $\binom{n}{k}$ as the number
of carries produced when $k$ and $n-k$ are added in base $p$; Lucas's theorem is its
congruence-theoretic twin.  We develop the corresponding theory for the Gaussian
($q$-)binomial coefficients $\qbin{n}{k}$, which for a prime power $q$ count the
$k$-dimensional subspaces of an $n$-dimensional vector space over the field with $q$ elements.

Fix an integer $q\ge 2$ and a prime $\ell\nmid q$.  Let $d$ be the multiplicative order of $q$
modulo $\ell$ (modulo $4$ when $\ell=2$) and put $e=v_\ell([d]_q)$, where
$[m]_q=1+q+\cdots+q^{m-1}$.  Our main theorem is the exact formula
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr)
   = e\,c + v_\ell\!\left(\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor}\right)
     + c\,v_\ell\!\left(\Bigl\lfloor \tfrac{n-k}{d}\Bigr\rfloor+1\right),
\]
where $c\in\{0,1\}$ is the carry out of the base-$d$ digit when $k$ and $n-k$ are added.
Combined with Kummer's classical theorem this exhibits the valuation as a carry count in the
mixed radix $(d,\ell,\ell,\dots)$: one anomalous digit of size $d$ and weight $e$, then ordinary
base-$\ell$ arithmetic.  We isolate the exact hypothesis the argument consumes, show that the
recipe fails at $\ell=2$ and that the order modulo $4$ repairs it, prove a $q$-analogue of Lucas's
theorem valid at every prime with no bound on $n$, derive closed-form survivor counts and a
complete description of the rows free of multiples of $\ell$, prove that the logarithmic growth
bound is attained infinitely often, and transport all statements to subspace counts in finite
vector spaces.
\end{abstract}

