\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The classical picture}
Two nineteenth-century theorems describe the arithmetic of binomial coefficients modulo a prime
$p$ with complete precision.  \emph{Kummer's theorem}: for $k\le n$, the $p$-adic valuation
$v_p\binom{n}{k}$ equals the number of carries when $k$ and $n-k$ are added in base $p$.
\emph{Lucas's theorem}: if $n=\sum n_ip^i$ and $k=\sum k_ip^i$ in base $p$, then
$\binom{n}{k}\equiv\prod_i\binom{n_i}{k_i}\pmod p$.  Together they explain the Sierpi\'nski
pattern of Pascal's triangle modulo $p$, Glaisher's count of surviving entries in a row, and the
bound $v_p\binom{n}{k}\le\log_p n$.

\subsection{The deformation}
Fix an integer $q\ge 2$ and set
\[
  [m]_q=\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}q^i=\frac{q^m-1}{q-1},\qquad
  [n]_q!=\prod_{m=1}^{n}[m]_q,\qquad
  \qbin{n}{k}=\frac{[n]_q!}{[k]_q!\,[n-k]_q!}.
\]
At $q=1$ these degenerate to $m$, $n!$, $\binom{n}{k}$.  For a prime power $q$ the Gaussian
coefficient counts the $k$-dimensional subspaces of $\FF_q^{\,n}$, so its arithmetic is the
arithmetic of a Grassmannian over a finite field.  The primes dividing $q$ are inert: every
$[m]_q$ is $\equiv 1$ modulo such a prime.  The content lies in the primes $\ell\nmid q$.

\subsection{Shape of the answer}
The deformation modifies \emph{exactly one digit}.  The base-$\ell$ expansion controlling the
classical theory acquires an extra, bottom digit of size $d=\ord_\ell(q)$ carrying weight
$e=v_\ell([d]_q)$; the higher digits are untouched.  Section~\ref{sec:val} proves this on the
valuation side, Section~\ref{sec:lucas} on the congruence side, Section~\ref{sec:count} in the
form of survivor counts, Section~\ref{sec:sharp} in the form of sharp extremal families, and
Section~\ref{sec:geom} in the geometric language of subspaces.  Section~\ref{sec:two} records the
anomaly at $\ell=2$ and its repair.

\section{Foundations: exactness of the $q$-Pascal recursion}

\begin{definition}[$q$-Pascal coefficient]\label{def:qpascal}
For $q\ge 0$ define $\qbin{n}{k}\in\mathbb{N}$ by
\[
  \qbin{n}{0}=1,\qquad \qbin{0}{k+1}=0,\qquad
  \qbin{n+1}{k+1}=\qbin{n}{k}+q^{\,k+1}\qbin{n}{k+1}.
\]
\end{definition}

Integrality and non-negativity are built in.

\begin{lemma}[Additivity]\label{lem:add}
$[a+b]_q=[a]_q+q^{a}[b]_q$, and $(q-1)[m]_q=q^m-1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split the geometric sum $\sum_{i<a+b}q^i$ at $i=a$ and factor out $q^a$; the second identity is
the telescoping sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness]\label{thm:exact}
For $k\le n$,
\[
  [k]_q!\;[n-k]_q!\;\qbin{n}{k}=[n]_q!.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$.  In the inductive step apply Definition~\ref{def:qpascal} and the two instances of
the inductive hypothesis at $(n,k)$ and $(n,k+1)$; the resulting terms recombine using
$[k+1]_q+q^{k+1}[n-k]_q=[n+1]_q$, which is Lemma~\ref{lem:add} with $a=k+1$, $b=n-k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\qbin{n}{k}=[n]_q!/([k]_q![n-k]_q!)$ exactly; $\qbin{n}{k}>0$ for $k\le n$; and
$\qbin{n}{k}=\qbin{n}{n-k}$.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} is the hinge of the paper: it converts every valuation question about
$\qbin{n}{k}$ into a counting question about which $q$-integers a prime divides, and how strongly.

\section{The valuation theory}\label{sec:val}

\begin{definition}[Regular datum]\label{def:reg}
Let $d>0$.  The pair $(d,e)$ is a \emph{regular datum for $q$ at $\ell$} if for every $m>0$
\[
  v_\ell([m]_q)=\begin{cases} e+v_\ell(m/d), & d\mid m,\\ 0,& d\nmid m.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

At $q=1$, $d=\ell$, $e=1$ this is the tautology underlying Legendre's formula.  Its virtue is that
the cyclotomic input (which $q$-integers $\ell$ divides) and the lifting-the-exponent input (how
strongly) are isolated once and for all.

\begin{theorem}[$q$-Legendre formula]\label{thm:legendre}
If $(d,e)$ is a regular datum for $q$ at $\ell$ then, for all $n$,
\[
  v_\ell\bigl([n]_q!\bigr)=e\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{d}\Bigr\rfloor
    +v_\ell\!\left(\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{d}\Bigr\rfloor!\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$ using $[n+1]_q!=[n+1]_q[n]_q!$.  If $d\mid n+1$ the floor increases by one and the
regular datum contributes $e+v_\ell(\lfloor (n+1)/d\rfloor)$, exactly the increment of the right
side; otherwise both sides are unchanged.
\end{proof}

Throughout write $N=\lfloor n/d\rfloor$, $A=\lfloor k/d\rfloor$, $B=\lfloor (n-k)/d\rfloor$, so
that $N=A+B+c$ with
\[
  c=\begin{cases}1,& (k\bmod d)+((n-k)\bmod d)\ge d,\\ 0,&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]

\begin{lemma}[Splitting a factorial along a carry]\label{lem:split}
For $c\le 1$,
\[
  v_\ell\bigl((A+B+c)!\bigr)=v_\ell\!\left(\binom{A+B+c}{A}\right)+v_\ell(A!)+v_\ell(B!)
    +c\,v_\ell(B+1).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply $\binom{M}{A}A!(M-A)!=M!$ with $M=A+B+c$; for $c=1$ the extra factor
$(B+1)!=(B+1)B!$ produces the last term.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$q$-Kummer, carry form]\label{thm:qkummer}
Let $(d,e)$ be a regular datum for $q$ at the prime $\ell$, let $k\le n$, and let $c\in\{0,1\}$
satisfy $N=A+B+c$.  Then
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr)= e\,c+v_\ell\!\left(\binom{N}{A}\right)+c\,v_\ell(B+1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $v_\ell$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} and substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:legendre} three times:
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr)+\bigl(eA+v_\ell(A!)\bigr)+\bigl(eB+v_\ell(B!)\bigr)
    = eN+v_\ell(N!).
\]
Expand $v_\ell(N!)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:split}, write $eN=eA+eB+ec$, and cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fully combinatorial form]\label{thm:carries}
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr)= e\,c
   +\#\bigl\{i\ge 1:\ \ell^{i}\le (A\bmod \ell^{i})+((N-A)\bmod \ell^{i})\bigr\}
   +c\,v_\ell(B+1),
\]
that is: $e$ times the base-$d$ carry, plus the number of carries when $A$ and $N-A$ are added in
base $\ell$, plus the correction term.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute Kummer's classical theorem for $v_\ell\binom{N}{A}$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:qkummer}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lte-prep}
For $q\ge2$ and a prime $\ell$: $\ord_\ell(q)\mid m$ if and only if $\ell\mid q^m-1$; and
$v_\ell(q^s-1)=v_\ell(q-1)+v_\ell([s]_q)$ for $s>0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Regularity at odd primes]\label{thm:odd}
Let $\ell$ be an odd prime, $q\ge2$, $\ell\nmid q$, $d=\ord_\ell(q)$ and $e=v_\ell([d]_q)$.  Then
$(d,e)$ is a regular datum for $q$ at $\ell$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $d\nmid m$ then $\ell\nmid q^m-1=(q-1)[m]_q$, so $v_\ell([m]_q)=0$.  If $m=dt$, apply lifting
the exponent to $x=q^{d}$: since $\ell$ is odd, $\ell\mid x-1$ and $\ell\nmid x$, one has
$v_\ell(x^{t}-1)=v_\ell(x-1)+v_\ell(t)$.  Rewriting both sides through
Lemma~\ref{lem:lte-prep} and cancelling $v_\ell(q-1)$ gives $v_\ell([dt]_q)=e+v_\ell(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[The test case]\label{ex:test}
$q=2$, $n=6$, $k=3$: $\qbin{6}{3}=1395=3^2\cdot5\cdot31$.  At $\ell=5$: $d=4$,
$e=v_5(15)=1$, $c=1$, $N=1$, $A=0$, $B=0$, so the formula predicts $1$, and $v_5(1395)=1$.  At
$\ell=3$: $d=2$, $e=v_3(3)=1$, $c=1$, $N=3$, $A=1$, $v_3\binom{3}{1}=1$, $v_3(B+1)=v_3(2)=0$,
predicting $2$, and $v_3(1395)=2$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[A pure base-$d$ carry count is false]\label{ex:false}
$q=2$, $\ell=5$, $d=4$, $e=1$, $n=16$, $k=1$.  In base $4$, $1+33_4=100_4$ carries twice, so a
naive count predicts $2$; but $\qbin{16}{1}=[16]_2=65535=3\cdot5\cdot17\cdot257$ has $v_5=1$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:qkummer} is correct: $c=1$ contributes $e=1$, while $N=4$, $A=0$ give
$v_5\binom{4}{0}=0$ and $v_5(B+1)=v_5(4)=0$.  Only the bottom digit is base $d$.
\end{example}

\section{The prime $\ell=2$: failure and repair}\label{sec:two}

\begin{proposition}[Failure of the naive recipe]\label{prop:fail2}
For odd $q$ the order of $q$ modulo $2$ is $1$, so the recipe of Theorem~\ref{thm:odd} proposes
the datum $(1,0)$, i.e.\ $v_2([m]_q)=v_2(m)$.  This is false: for $q=3$, $[2]_3=4$ has $v_2=2$
while the recipe predicts $1$.  Consequently the naive $q$-Kummer prediction fails as well:
$\qbin{2}{1}=4$ at $q=3$ has $v_2=2$, whereas $v_2\binom{2}{1}=1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:par}
For odd $q$: $[m]_q\equiv m\pmod 2$, so $v_2([m]_q)=0$ for odd $m$; and for even $m\neq 0$ with
$q\ge3$, $v_2([m]_q)+1=v_2(q+1)+v_2(m)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Repair at $\ell=2$]\label{thm:two}
Let $q\ge2$ be odd.  If $q\equiv1\pmod 4$ then $(1,0)$ is a regular datum for $q$ at $2$.  If
$q\equiv3\pmod4$ then $\bigl(2,\;v_2([2]_q)\bigr)=\bigl(2,\;v_2(q+1)\bigr)$ is a regular datum.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Odd $m$ is handled by Proposition~\ref{prop:par}.  For even $m$ apply the lifting statement of the
same proposition: when $q\equiv1\pmod4$ one has $v_2(q+1)=1$, giving $v_2([m]_q)=v_2(m)$; when
$q\equiv3\pmod4$ write $m=2t$, so $v_2([2t]_q)=v_2(q+1)+v_2(t)$.
\end{proof}

In both cases the correct period is the multiplicative order of $q$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/4)^{\times}$.

\begin{definition}[Period and offset]\label{def:period}
For $q\ge2$ and a prime $\ell\nmid q$ put
\[
  D(q,\ell)=\begin{cases}\ord_4(q),&\ell=2,\\ \ord_\ell(q),&\ell\ \text{odd},\end{cases}
  \qquad E(q,\ell)=v_\ell\bigl([D(q,\ell)]_q\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Master theorem]\label{thm:master}
For every $q\ge2$ and every prime $\ell\nmid q$, the pair $(D(q,\ell),E(q,\ell))$ is a regular
datum; hence for all $k\le n$, writing $D=D(q,\ell)$, $E=E(q,\ell)$ and $c$ for the base-$D$ carry,
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr)
    = E\,c+v_\ell\!\left(\binom{\lfloor n/D\rfloor}{\lfloor k/D\rfloor}\right)
      +c\,v_\ell\!\left(\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n-k}{D}\Bigr\rfloor+1\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

The boundary is sharp in both directions: Proposition~\ref{prop:fail2} rules out the order modulo
$2$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:two} shows the order modulo $4$ suffices.

\section{A $q$-analogue of Lucas's theorem}\label{sec:lucas}

\begin{definition}[$q$-Lucas period]\label{def:qlucas}
A positive integer $d$ is a \emph{$q$-Lucas period for the prime $\ell$} if
(i) $\ell\mid[d]_q$; (ii) $q^{d}\equiv1\pmod\ell$; (iii) $\ell\nmid[i]_q$ for $0<i<d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
If $q\ge2$, $\ell\nmid q$ is prime and $d=\ord_\ell(q)>1$, then $d$ is a $q$-Lucas period for
$\ell$.  In the degenerate case $d=1$ one has $[m]_q\equiv m$ and $\qbin{n}{k}\equiv\binom{n}{k}
\pmod\ell$.
\end{proposition}

Let $\Red_d(n)=\prod_{m\le n,\ d\nmid m}[m]_q$ denote the $d$-free part of the $q$-factorial.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:blocks}
$[dj]_q=[d]_q\,[j]_{q^{d}}$, and consequently
\[
  [n]_q!=\Red_d(n)\cdot[d]_q^{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}\cdot\bigl[\lfloor n/d\rfloor\bigr]_{q^{d}}!.
\]
Moreover, if $d$ is a $q$-Lucas period for $\ell$, then modulo $\ell$ one has
$[m]_q\equiv[m\bmod d]_q$ and
$\Red_d(n)\equiv[d-1]_q!^{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}[n\bmod d]_q!$; in particular $[m]_q$ is a unit
modulo $\ell$ whenever $d\nmid m$, and $\qbin{r}{s}$ is a unit modulo $\ell$ for $s\le r<d$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$q$-Lucas]\label{thm:qlucas}
Let $d$ be a $q$-Lucas period for the prime $\ell$.  Then for all $k\le n$,
\[
  \qbin{n}{k}\;\equiv\;\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor}\cdot
    \qbin{n\bmod d}{k\bmod d}\pmod{\ell}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Feed the three-factor splitting of Lemma~\ref{lem:blocks} for $n$, $k$ and $n-k$ into
Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}.  Two exact identities over $\mathbb{N}$ result, according to whether the
base-$d$ digits carry:
\[
  \Red(k)\Red(n-k)\qbin{n}{k}=\Red(n)\genfrac{[}{]}{0pt}{}{N}{A}_{q^{d}}
\]
in the carry-free case, and
\[
  \Red(k)\Red(n-k)\qbin{n}{k}
    =\Red(n)\,[d]_q\,[N]_{q^{d}}\genfrac{[}{]}{0pt}{}{N-1}{A}_{q^{d}}
\]
in the carry case.  Every $\Red$-factor is a unit modulo $\ell$, and since $q^{d}\equiv1$ the
$q^{d}$-binomial coefficient degenerates to the classical $\binom{N}{A}$; cancelling units gives
the congruence.  In the carry case the explicit factor $[d]_q$ is divisible by $\ell$, so the left
side vanishes, and so does the right side because $\qbin{r}{s}=0$ for $s>r$.
\end{proof}

Two features deserve emphasis.  No parity hypothesis is needed: the proof uses only that
$\mathbb{Z}/\ell$ is a field, so the congruence holds at $\ell=2$ with the naive period.  And no
bound on $n$ is needed: routing the block product through the $q^{d}$-binomial coefficient removes
the restriction $\lfloor n/d\rfloor<\ell$ that a naive cancellation would demand.  For instance
$q=2$, $\ell=3$, $n=13$, $k=6$: here $d=2$, $N=6\ge\ell$, and
$\qbin{13}{6}=14877590196755\equiv\binom{6}{3}=20\equiv2\pmod3$.

\begin{theorem}[Mixed-radix form]\label{thm:mixed}
If in addition $\lfloor n/d\rfloor<\ell^{a}$, then
\[
  \qbin{n}{k}\equiv\qbin{n\bmod d}{k\bmod d}\cdot
    \prod_{i<a}\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor/\ell^{i}\bmod\ell}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor/\ell^{i}\bmod\ell}
  \pmod{\ell}.
\]
\end{theorem}

Exactly one deformed factor survives---the one attached to the anomalous radix $d$---and all the
rest are classical binomial coefficients of single base-$\ell$ digits.

\section{Counting survivors}\label{sec:count}

Call $\qbin{n}{k}$ a \emph{survivor} at $\ell$ if $\ell\nmid\qbin{n}{k}$.

\begin{theorem}[Indivisibility criterion]\label{thm:crit}
Let $d$ be a $q$-Lucas period for $\ell$ and $k\le n$.  Then
\[
  \ell\nmid\qbin{n}{k}\iff
    \Bigl(k\bmod d\le n\bmod d\ \text{ and }\
      \ell\nmid\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor}\Bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:qlucas} the reduction is a product of two factors.  If $k\bmod d>n\bmod d$ the
residual factor is $\qbin{r}{s}$ with $s>r$, hence zero; otherwise it is a unit
(Lemma~\ref{lem:blocks}), so the product vanishes precisely when the classical factor does.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Row count]\label{thm:rowcount}
With $d$ a $q$-Lucas period for $\ell$,
\[
  \#\Bigl\{k\le n:\ \ell\nmid\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr\}
   =(n\bmod d+1)\cdot\#\Bigl\{A\le\lfloor n/d\rfloor:\
       \ell\nmid\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{A}\Bigr\}
   =(n\bmod d+1)\prod_i\bigl(\delta_i+1\bigr),
\]
where $\delta_i$ are the base-$\ell$ digits of $\lfloor n/d\rfloor$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $k=dA+s$ with $0\le s<d$; Theorem~\ref{thm:crit} splits the condition into one on $s$ alone
(exactly $n\bmod d+1$ admissible values) and one on $A$ alone.  The classical factor is Glaisher's
count $\#\{A\le N: p\nmid\binom{N}{A}\}=\prod_i(N_i+1)$, itself proved by the same blockwise
recursion from the one-step Lucas criterion.
\end{proof}

The residual factor is $n\bmod d+1$, \emph{not} $d-(n\bmod d)$: the surviving residues are those
at most the last base-$d$ digit of $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact self-similar total]\label{thm:total}
For every $m\ge0$,
\[
  \sum_{n<d\,\ell^{m}}\#\Bigl\{k\le n:\ \ell\nmid\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr\}
   =\Bigl(\sum_{r<d}(r+1)\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{t<\ell}(t+1)\Bigr)^{m}
   =\frac{d(d+1)}{2}\left(\frac{\ell(\ell+1)}{2}\right)^{m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Group rows as $n=Md+j$ with $j<d$ and use Theorem~\ref{thm:rowcount}; the classical identity
$\sum_{N<\ell^{m}}\prod_i(N_i+1)=\bigl(\sum_{t<\ell}(t+1)\bigr)^{m}$ follows by the same blockwise
induction.
\end{proof}

The exponential growth rate is entirely classical; the deformation contributes the constant factor
$d(d+1)/2$, the number of cells of one triangular block.

\begin{theorem}[Full rows]\label{thm:full}
For a prime $p$, the $N$-th row of Pascal's triangle contains no multiple of $p$ if and only if
$N+1=c\,p^{t}$ for some $1\le c\le p$.  Consequently, for a $q$-Lucas period $d$ of $\ell$, the
$n$-th row of the $q$-Pascal triangle consists entirely of integers prime to $\ell$ if and only if
\[
  n+1\le d \qquad\text{or}\qquad n+1=d\,c\,\ell^{t}\ \text{ for some } 1\le c\le\ell,\ t\ge0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction using the one-step criterion: for $N\ge p$ the classical row is full iff
$N\bmod p=p-1$ and the row $\lfloor N/p\rfloor$ is full.  For the deformed statement, apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:crit}: the $n$-th row is full iff $n<d$, or $n\bmod d=d-1$ and the block row
$\lfloor n/d\rfloor$ is classically full.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Residual entry]\label{prop:residual}
Let $(d,e)$ be a regular datum.  If $n\ge d$ and $n\bmod d<d-1$, then the entry
$k=n\bmod d+1$ forces a base-$d$ carry and
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{\,n\bmod d+1}\Bigr)=e+v_\ell\bigl(\lfloor n/d\rfloor\bigr)\ \ge\ e .
\]
\end{proposition}

Thus a row is either full, or contains an entry of valuation at least $e$: the excluded residue
$n\bmod d=d-1$ is exactly the one appearing in the full-row criterion.

\section{Growth, sharpness and self-similarity}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]\label{thm:bound}
For a regular datum $(d,e)$ and $k\le n$,
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{n}{k}\Bigr)\le e+\log_\ell\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{d}\Bigr\rfloor
    +\log_\ell\Bigl(\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n-k}{d}\Bigr\rfloor+1\Bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharpness}
Let $(d,e)$ be a regular datum with $d\ge2$.  Then for every $s\ge1$,
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{d\,\ell^{s}}{\,d+1}\Bigr)=e+s .
\]
In particular the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:bound} is attained infinitely often, and the
valuations are unbounded.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $n=d\ell^{s}$, $k=d+1$.  Then $k\bmod d=1$ and $n-k=d(\ell^{s}-2)+(d-1)$, so
$(n-k)\bmod d=d-1$ and the base-$d$ addition carries, $c=1$.  The block indices are $N=\ell^{s}$,
$A=1$, $B=\ell^{s}-2$, whence $v_\ell\binom{\ell^{s}}{1}=s$ and $v_\ell(B+1)=v_\ell(\ell^{s}-1)=0$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:qkummer} gives $e+s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Self-similarity]\label{thm:selfsim}
For a regular datum $(d,e)$ and $A\le N$,
\[
  v_\ell\Bigl(\qbin{dN}{dA}\Bigr)=v_\ell\!\left(\binom{N}{A}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both $dA$ and $d(N-A)$ are multiples of $d$, so $c=0$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:qkummer} collapses to
the classical term.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divisibility criterion, valuation form]
For a regular datum $(d,e)$ with $e\ge1$ and $k\le n$, one has $\ell\mid\qbin{n}{k}$ if and only if
$c=1$ or $\ell\mid\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor}$.  For an odd prime $\ell\nmid q$
with $d=\ord_\ell(q)>1$, the hypothesis $e\ge1$ is automatic.
\end{theorem}

\section{Application: subspaces of finite vector spaces}\label{sec:geom}

For a prime power $q$ the number of $k$-dimensional subspaces of an $n$-dimensional
$\FF_q$-vector space is
\[
  \Gr_q(n,k)=\frac{\prod_{i<k}(q^{n}-q^{i})}{\prod_{i<k}(q^{k}-q^{i})}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Identification]\label{thm:bridge}
For $q\ge2$ and $k\le n$, $\Gr_q(n,k)=\qbin{n}{k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By induction on $k$, using $q^{n}-q^{i}=q^{i}(q-1)[n-i]_q$, one gets
$\prod_{i<k}(q^{n}-q^{i})\,[n-k]_q!=q^{\binom{k}{2}}(q-1)^{k}[n]_q!$; specialising $n=k$ evaluates
the denominator, and dividing, together with Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}, gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Valuation of subspace counts]\label{thm:subspaces}
Let $K$ be a finite field with $q$ elements, $V$ a $K$-vector space of dimension $n$, and $\ell$ an
odd prime with $\ell\nmid q$.  With $d=\ord_\ell(q)$, $e=v_\ell([d]_q)$ and $c$ the base-$d$ carry
of $k$ and $n-k$,
\[
  v_\ell\bigl(\#\{W\le V:\ \dim W=k\}\bigr)
   = e\,c+v_\ell\!\left(\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor}\right)
     +c\,v_\ell\!\left(\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n-k}{d}\Bigr\rfloor+1\right).
\]
Moreover, with no parity restriction,
$\#\{W\le V:\dim W=k\}\equiv\binom{\lfloor n/d\rfloor}{\lfloor k/d\rfloor}
\qbin{n\bmod d}{k\bmod d}\pmod\ell$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
$V=\FF_2^{6}$, $k=3$: there are $1395=3^2\cdot5\cdot31$ three-dimensional subspaces, and the
theorem predicts $v_3=2$, $v_5=1$, $v_{31}=1$.  For $\ell=31$: $d=\ord_{31}(2)=5$,
$e=v_{31}([5]_2)=v_{31}(31)=1$, $3\bmod5+3\bmod5=6\ge5$ so $c=1$, and the remaining terms vanish.
\end{example}

These statements are precisely what is needed in the $\ell$-modular representation theory of
$\mathrm{GL}_n(\FF_q)$ in non-defining characteristic $\ell\nmid q$, where the order
$d=\ord_\ell(q)$ already organises the unipotent blocks.

\section{Algorithms}

\emph{Gaussian coefficients.}  The recursion of Definition~\ref{def:qpascal} computes
$\qbin{n}{k}$ in $O(n^{2})$ big-integer operations; the entries have $\Theta(k(n-k)\log q)$ bits.

\emph{Valuation.}  Compute $D$ by repeated multiplication modulo $\ell$ (or modulo $4$), the
offset $E$ by trial division, the carry $c$ by comparing residues, and the classical term by
counting base-$\ell$ carries.  Cost $O(\ell+\log_\ell n)$ operations---polynomial in $\log n$ once
$D,E$ are cached, versus exponential for forming the coefficient itself.

\emph{Residue.}  Theorem~\ref{thm:mixed} computes $\qbin{n}{k}\bmod\ell$ in $O(D^{2}+\log_\ell n)$
operations modulo $\ell$, hence is feasible for astronomically large $n$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The unifying statement is that passing from $q=1$ to general $q$ splices a single new digit, of
size $d=\ord_\ell(q)$ and weight $e=v_\ell([d]_q)$, into the bottom of the base-$\ell$ expansion
governing the classical theory.  Valuations become mixed-radix carry counts
(Theorem~\ref{thm:carries}); congruences become mixed-radix digit products with exactly one
deformed factor (Theorem~\ref{thm:mixed}); the Sierpi\'nski pattern is dilated by $d$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:rowcount}); the total survivor count acquires one constant factor $d(d+1)/2$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:total}); the full rows are $d$-dilates of the classical ones plus $d$ initial
rows (Theorem~\ref{thm:full}); the growth bound gains the additive constant $e$ and is sharp
(Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpness}); and along multiples of $d$ the classical theory is recovered exactly
(Theorem~\ref{thm:selfsim}).

The correction term $c\,v_\ell(B+1)$ has no classical shadow but is not slack: it records the extra
factor $B+1$ pushed into the block factorial by a base-$d$ carry.  The valuation and congruence
theories part company at $\ell=2$: the former needs lifting the exponent and hence the order modulo
$4$, while the latter needs only that $\mathbb{Z}/\ell$ is a field.  It is the \emph{strength} of
divisibility, not divisibility itself, that is $2$-adically delicate.

Natural continuations include: congruences modulo $\ell^{r}$ in the style of Granville; an
intrinsic cyclotomic reformulation with $e=v_\ell(\Phi_d(q))$; the case of $q$ a root of unity in a
local field; $q$-multinomial coefficients counting flags; the exact renormalisation limit and
Hausdorff dimension of the deformed fractal; applications to block theory for
$\mathrm{GL}_n(\FF_q)$ in non-defining characteristic; and the inverse problem of describing all
$(n,k)$ with a prescribed valuation, of which Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpness} settles the extremal case.

